  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nIn line with the prior forecast update, this one maintains the\nearlier arrival time of a cold front, sweeping down across the \narea this afternoon and evening, before stalling tonight. \nProvided that southwesterly surface flow through much of the \nday will keep dew points >70F with 60% to 90% confidence and \ntemperatures climbing into the mid to upper 80s, the \nthermodynamic setup will feel more like summer than fall. Ascent\naloft seems to be subtle during the day, which might hint to \nconvective chances being mostly tied to the front itself (north \nof I-70), though warm and moist conditions with 500-1500 J/kg \nSBCAPE (25th-75th percentile), and broad ascent mean pop up \nstorms could theoretically develop anywhere. Sfc-6km shear is \nfairly weak, though most of this is packed into the sfc-2km \nlayer, meaning a spin up couldn't be ruled out. That being said,\nmost of this is straight-line and westerly, so any rotation \nwould be dependent on a right moving (southeast moving) cell. \nDCAPE will be more marginal, with 500-700 J/kg forecast, most \nreliant on deep vertical development to realize gusty wind \npotential. ML Outlooks are not very bullish at on on severe \npotential, with both right movers and deep storms generally \nunlikely, but the severe risk is non-zero nonetheless. What may \nbe more likely is localized flood in the event of any training \nthat might occur (particularly for urbanized corridors), with \nPWATS pushing 1.8-2.0 inches (above the daily record) and warm \nrainfall processes leading to efficient, heavy rainfall in and \nstorms that do develop.\n\n00Z models have been more consistent with a second round of \nstorms developing upstream early Thursday morning, as a mid- \nlevel shortwave accelerates low level flow above the nocturnal \ninversion. Sfc-3km shear may increase to 20-30kts, with >90% \nchance of non-zero MUCAPE with the 90th percentile as high as \n1000 J/kg. Convective chances have increased, but timing still \nremains uncertain. Most ensembles have these storms progressing \ninto the area around 6am Thursday, but outliers (NAM), show \ndevelopment potential as early as midnight. Should this occur, \nsevere risk will be low with decoupling, but PWATS will remain \nabove record levels (1.9-2.1 inches), so localized flash flood \npotential lingers for training storms or areas already hit from \nthe day prior. In the slowest scenarios for this passage, a very\nlow severe risk (weak spin up / winds) lingers for west PA / \nnorth WV early Thursday should the atmosphere decouple late \nmorning. Coverage during the afternoon Thursday has trended down\nbehind this morning wave, but still non-zero storm chances \nlinger for the day (though severe and flood risks may be lower\nthan the prior update). Storm coverage Friday has also trended \nlower with the only chance of rain/storms south of I-70 in the \nevent the front hasn't fully cleared yet.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA parade of shortwaves in WNW flow aloft allude to more chances\nof rain and thunder by the weekend. Confidence remains highest\nlate Saturday into Monday (75% chance of rain). This will carry\nmore low-end severe/flood risks. \n\nThe pattern by the middle of next week remains uncertain, with\none camp of ensembles remaining unsettled and seasonable, in a \npattern similar to that of this weekend. Another camp bring a \ncold front through, with cooler and drier conditions.",
